/P <<
/ActualText (The chart shows the past performance and price changes of \044HD for five years\056 You can see the stock\220s per\055share trading price in dollars on the vertical axis\056 At the bottom of almost every single stock chart\054 you will see time\056 In this chart\054 we see labels that span out across five years\056 The amount of time your chart covers heavily influences your trading style\056 Across the bottom of the chart\054 we see \215D\216 for dividend\054 representing any time when this stock paid dividends to shareholders \050i\056e\056 the company pays you from their earnings to own their stock\056 We will further explore dividends in Chapter 15\051\056\271 Also across the bottom of this chart\054 you can see red and green volume bars\056 These bars are red when the price decreases from the trading period prior and green when the price increases\056\262 As a general rule of thumb\054 increased volume \050how many shares of a stock are traded in any given period\051 usually means there is more attention)
