/ActualText (Now that you are familiar with dividends which show up in Yahoo Finance charts as big \215D\216s\054 let\220s looks at stock splits which show up at the bottom of charts as subtle \215S\216s\056\271 A company may split its stock when it wants to lower its per\055share price for smaller investors\054 reward executives with more shares before new financing rounds\054 etc\056\262 The principle behind these splits is existing shareholders\220 positions are multiplied by X shares per existing share\054 and the stock price is divided by that same number X\056\262 For instance\054 say a company\220s stock trades at \044100 and you own ten shares of the company\056 A two\055to\055one split would reward you with twenty shares of stock\054 and the company\220s trading price would go to \04450\056 The number of shares you own doubles\054 and the price of the stock halved\056\263 Stock splits like these and reverse splits\054 which we will explore soon\054 affect all market shareholders by changing the number of tradable shares available in the underlying company\056)
/MCID 2
